Add one or more rules to a security group.

USAGE:
  scw instance security-group add-rules <security-group-id ...> [arg=value ...]

ARGS:
  security-group-id                         ID of the security group to add rules to
  [rules.{index}.protocol]                  Protocol for the rule (unknown_protocol | tcp | udp | icmp | any)
  [rules.{index}.direction]                 Direction of traffic for the rule (unknown_direction | inbound | outbound | both)
  [rules.{index}.action]                    Action to take when the rule matches (unknown_action | accept | drop)
  [rules.{index}.source-ip-range]           Source IP range for the rule
  [rules.{index}.destination-ip-range]      Destination IP range for the rule
  [rules.{index}.source-ports.start]        Start of the port range
  [rules.{index}.source-ports.end]          End of the port range
  [rules.{index}.destination-ports.start]   Start of the port range
  [rules.{index}.destination-ports.end]     End of the port range
  [rules.{index}.position]                  Position of this rule in the rule list, starting at 1
  [zone=fr-par-1]                           Zone to target. If none is passed will use default zone from the config (fr-par-1 | fr-par-2 | fr-par-3 | nl-ams-1 | nl-ams-2 | nl-ams-3 | pl-waw-1 | pl-waw-2 | pl-waw-3 | it-mil-1 | it-mil-2 | it-mil-3)

FLAGS:
  -h, --help                help for add-rules
      --list-sub-commands   List all subcommands

GLOBAL FLAGS:
  -B, --beta             Enable beta mode
  -c, --config string    The path to the config file
  -D, --debug            Enable debug mode
  -H, --no-headers       Skip headers in human output
  -o, --output string    Output format: json or human, see 'scw help output' for more info (default "human")
  -p, --profile string   The config profile to use
      --web              open console page for the current resource
